  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA decent low level jet will develop Sunday night across the \nwestern 2/3rds of the forecast area. Based on Bufkit forecast \nsoundings, mixing will continue well into the late evening hours\nSunday. This will lead to a slow cooldown Sunday evening and \nprolong fire weather concerns across the western half of the \nforecast area. More about that in the fire weather section \nbelow. With winds not expected to totally decouple Sunday night,\nlow temperatures will be very mild across the forecast area \nwith readings in the middle 60s to around 70. A cold front will \nbegin to approach northwestern Nebraska midday drifting slowly \neast and southeast Monday afternoon. There are some minor \ndifferences in frontal location at 21z Monday with the GFS \nhaving this feature oriented along a line from around Oshkosh to\nValentine. The NAM is fairly close with this feature placing \nthe front along a line from Chappell to Springview. Highs south \nof the front will reach the middle to upper 90s with lower 90s \nforecast north of the front. There will be a minor threat for \nshowers and thunderstorms along the front Monday afternoon/early\nevening. Better chances for showers and thunderstorms will \narrive behind the front Monday night into Tuesday morning in \nadvance of a decent mid level shortwave. The latest NBM \nprobabilistic forecast has a 40 to 70% chance of 12 hr QPF > \n0.01 inches across the FA ending at 12z Tuesday. When the QPF \nthreshold is raised to 0.10\", percentages of exceedance fall off\nto 40 percent over northern Nebraska. Going forward 12 hours, \nending at 00z Wednesday, there is a 60 percent chance of \nexceeding 0.10\" over northern Nebraska with dry conditions over \nsouthwestern into the western Sandhills. That being said, the \nhighest pops will be placed over northern portions of the \nforecast area with lower pops further south and west for the \nremainder of the FA. The threat for precipitation will carry \nover into Wednesday as a second upper level trough approaches \nthe western Dakotas and the panhandle early Wednesday morning. \nThis feature will track across the Dakotas and northern Nebraska\nWednesday morning into the afternoon hours bringing another \nthreat for precipitation, mainly over the northern half of the \nforecast area. With the expected threat for precipitation \nTuesday and Wednesday, highs will struggle to get out of the 70s\nover the northern half of the forecast area. Further south, \nreadings may make it into the lower 80s. Broad zonal flow aloft \nwill develop across the central and northern plains toward the \nend of next week. Readings will rebound back into the 80s and \nlower 90s with a limited threat for precipitation.",
